Reading this table.
  /incumbent is formed inside this process against the gp arm of the SAME set,
    which is the only comparison that means anything across sets: the payloads
    are different sizes, so an absolute moves for that reason alone.
  `x its own ascii` is R4's within-arm form: each arm against ITSELF on ascii.
    The Rust slice found that a content-set finding expressed this way
    reproduces almost exactly where the ratio to a third arm drifts.
  The wire grows with the set, so part of `x its own ascii` is simply more
    bytes. The correctness table above gives the width factor per payload, and
    an arm whose time grows FASTER than its bytes is paying for the transcode.
